{"summary": "The design is coherent and consistent — participants, operations and requirement coverage all check out — but the runtime wiring is under-specified. The biggest gap is that no diagram shows startup and persistence (schema creation, category seeding, repository injection), which is exactly what REQ-007 depends on.", "findings": [{"severity": "high", "diagram": "all", "finding": "REQ-007 is only covered on paper: AppDatabase.create_schema() and CategoryRepository.seed_defaults() exist in the class diagram but no sequence shows the startup path (main → TrackerServer.start() → schema creation → seeding → repositories wired into ExpenseTrackerService → ApiHandler), nor how a restart re-reads the SQLite file.", "suggestion": "Add a startup sequence diagram: `python -m expensetracker` → main builds AppDatabase → create_schema() → CategoryRepository.seed_defaults() → constructs the three repositories and ExpenseTrackerService → TrackerServer(port=8000, handler=ApiHandler(service)).start(), and note that the DB file path (data/expense_tracker.db) is created if missing."}, {"severity": "medium", "diagram": "Domain classes", "finding": "The SQLite connection lifecycle is undefined: AppDatabase.connect() returns a Connection but nothing says whether one shared connection lives in AppDatabase for the whole process or each request opens its own, and the component diagram leaves the single- vs multi-threaded HTTP server open, which decides whether a shared sqlite3 connection is safe.", "suggestion": "State it explicitly in the class diagram (e.g. AppDatabase opens one connection per call / per request with a `with` block, or a single connection guarded by a lock) and say in the component diagram whether ThreadingHTTPServer or HTTPServer is used."}, {"severity": "medium", "diagram": "Log expense", "finding": "The sequence re-fetches the whole list (GET /api/expenses → list_newest_first) after saving, while the activity diagram says the new row is appended to the list; the full refetch also drags all 10,000 stored expenses over the wire before the confirmation, which conflicts with REQ-009.", "suggestion": "Pick one behaviour: return the saved expense (and its display strings) in the 201 response and have TrackerPage insert the row at the right position after renderExpenses, or explicitly document the refetch as intended and bound it (e.g. a limit on list_expenses)."}, {"severity": "medium", "diagram": "Budget overview", "finding": "ExpenseRepository.sum_by_category is shown returning dicts keyed by category name ({Groceries: 8500}), but the note in the summary diagram says the query does `SUM(amount_cents) GROUP BY category_id`; the repository has no join to the category table, so the two diagrams disagree on the key type.", "suggestion": "Define the return type once (e.g. `dict[int, int]` keyed by category_id, joined to Category in the service for names) or change the SQL note to `JOIN categories ... GROUP BY c.name`, and use the same shape in both sequences."}, {"severity": "medium", "diagram": "all", "finding": "The current month is read from the wall clock inside budget_overview() and monthly_summary(), which take no month argument, so the month in `sum_by_category(2026, 5)` cannot be fixed in a test; REQ-005/REQ-006 boundary cases (previous month excluded) are then untestable without freezing time.", "suggestion": "Inject a `Clock.today()` (or `month`) into ExpenseTrackerService and add an optional `month` parameter to monthly_summary(month=None) / budget_overview(month=None) defaulting to the clock, so tests can pass 2026-05 explicitly."}, {"severity": "medium", "diagram": "Expense validation flow", "finding": "Validation responsibility is split without being assigned: the activity diagram shows amount/date parsing and a `<= 0` check as bare steps, but the class diagram has no date-parsing operation and Money.parse_amount returns an int, so it is unclear whether Money or the service rejects missing/non-positive amounts and bad dates.", "suggestion": "Give Money.parse_amount the full contract (raise ValidationError('amount', …) for missing, non-numeric or <= 0) and add a `Dates.parse_date(text) : date` helper (or a FormValidator) so the activity steps map to real operations."}, {"severity": "low", "diagram": "Use cases", "finding": "The include/extend arrows are wrong or misleading: `UC5 ..> UC4 : <<extend>>` points from the base use case to the extension, and `Log an expense ..> View recorded expenses <<include>>` and `budget usage ..> summary <<include>>` claim inclusions that are really optional follow-ups.", "suggestion": "Reverse the extend arrow (`UC4 ..> UC5`) or drop it, and remove the include arrows that are not mandatory parts of the base case, keeping only real mandatory inclusions."}, {"severity": "low", "diagram": "Domain classes", "finding": "list_newest_first() is defined only as 'ordering by spent_on', so expenses saved on the same date come back in unspecified order and REQ-003's 'newest first' is only partially guaranteed; the index needed for that ordering and for the month filter (REQ-009) is also absent from create_schema().", "suggestion": "Document `ORDER BY spent_on DESC, id DESC` for list_newest_first() and have create_schema() create `INDEX ON expenses(spent_on)` (and on category_id) so the month aggregation stays fast with 10,000 rows."}]}
