dget overview: ExpenseRepository.sum_by_category is shown returning dicts keyed by category name ({Groceries: 8500}), but the note in the summary diagram says the query does `SUM(amount_cents) GROUP BY category_id`; the repository has no join to the category table, so the two diagrams disagree on the key type. Suggestion: Define the return type once (e.g. `dict[int, int]` keyed by category_id, joined to Category in the service for names) or change the SQL note to `JOIN categories ... GROUP BY c.name`, and use the same shape in both sequences.\nF5 [medium] all: The current month is read from the wall clock inside budget_overview() and monthly_summary(), which take no month argument, so the month in `sum_by_category(2026, 5)` cannot be fixed in a test; REQ-005/REQ-006 boundary cases (previous month excluded) are then untestable without freezing time. Suggestion: Inject a `Clock.today()` (or `month`) into ExpenseTrackerService and add an optional `month` parameter to monthly_summary(month=None) / budget_overview(month=None) defaulting to the clock, so tests can pass 2026-05 explicitly.\nF6 [medium] Expense validation flow: Validation responsibility is split without being assigned: the activity diagram shows amount/date parsing and a `<= 0` check as bare steps, but the class diagram has no date-parsing operation and Money.parse_amount returns an int, so it is unclear whether Money or the service rejects missing/non-positive amounts and bad dates. Suggestion: Give Money.parse_amount the full contract (raise ValidationError('amount', …) for missing, non-numeric or <= 0) and add a `Dates.parse_date(text) : date` helper (or a FormValidator) so the activity steps map to real operations."
