      "content": "Approved requirements:\n# Requirements\n\nA single-user personal expense tracker that runs in the browser, served locally by Python. It records expenses by amount, date and category, tracks monthly budgets per category with near-limit warnings, and shows monthly spending by category; data persists across restarts.\n\n## Functional requirements\n\n### REQ-001 Local web app served by Python (must)\n\nThe expense tracker runs in the browser as a web app served locally by `python -m expensetracker`. It uses only the Python 3.12 standard library and needs no network access beyond the local machine.\n\n- Given the app is not running, when the developer runs `python -m expensetracker`, then the server starts and the tracker page is reachable in a browser on the local machine.\n- Given the tracker page is open, when the user uses the app, then expenses, budgets and summaries are served by the local Python server without contacting any external service.\n\n### REQ-002 Log an expense (must)\n\nThe user can record an expense with an amount, a date and a category chosen from the available categories. The expense is saved and shown in the expense list.\n\n- Given the expense form, when the user submits amount 12.50, date 2026-05-03 and category Groceries, then the expense is saved and appears in the expense list.\n- Given the expense form, when the user submits a missing, non-numeric or non-positive amount, then an error message is shown and no expense is saved.\n- Given the expense form, when the user submits a date that is not a valid calendar date, then an error message is shown and no expense is saved.\n\n### REQ-003 View recorded expenses (must)\n\nThe app shows the recorded expenses in a list, newest first, each with its amount, date and category.\n\n- Given three saved expenses with different dates, when the expense list is displayed, then all three appear ordered by date from newest to oldest with amount, date and category shown.\n- Given no expenses have been saved, when the expense list is displayed, then an empty-state message is shown instead of an error.\n\n### REQ-004 Set a monthly budget per category (must)\n\nThe user can set or update a monthly spending budget for each category. The budget is stored and shown next to that category.\n\n- Given no budget exists for Groceries, when the user sets a budget of 300.00, then the budget is saved and displayed for Groceries.\n- Given a budget of 300.00 for Groceries, when the user changes it to 350.00, then the stored and displayed budget is 350.00 and the earlier value is no longer used.\n- Given the budget form, when the user enters a negative or non-numeric budget, then an error message is shown and no budget is saved.\n\n### REQ-005 Near-limit and over-limit budget warnings (must)\n\nFor each category with a budget, the app shows how much of the budget this month's spending has used and warns when spending reaches 80% of the limit, and flags the category as over budget when spending exceeds 100%.\n\n- Given a Groceries budget of 100.00 and 85.00 spent this month, when the budget view is shown, then Groceries is marked as close to its limit.\n- Given a Groceries budget of 100.00 and 120.00 spent this month, when the budget view is shown, then Groceries is marked as over budget.\n- Given a Groceries budget of 100.00 and 40.00 spent this month, when the budget view is shown, then Groceries is shown without a warning.\n\n### REQ-006 Monthly spending summary by category (must)\n\nThe app shows the total amount spent in the current calendar month and the same total broken down by category. Expenses from other months are excluded.\n\n- Given expenses of 40.00 in Groceries and 60.00 in Transport in the current month, when the summary is shown, then the monthly total is 100.00 with Groceries 40.00 and Transport 60.00.\n- Given an expense dated in a previous month, when the current month's summary is shown, then that expense is not included in the total or the category breakdown.\n\n### REQ-007 Data persists across restarts (must)\n\nExpenses and budgets are stored in a local SQLite database file so they are still available after the server is stopped and started again.\n\n- Given saved expenses and budgets, when the server is stopped and `python -m expensetracker` is started again, then all previously saved expenses and budgets are shown.\n- Given no database file exists, when the app starts for the first time, then it creates the storage file and starts with an empty expense list.\n\n## Non-functional requirements\n\n### REQ-008 Exact money handling (must)\n\nMonetary amounts are stored exactly, without floating-point rounding drift, and are displayed with two decimal places.\n\n- Given expenses of 0.10 and 0.20 in the same category, when the summary is shown, then the category total is displayed as 0.30.\n- Given a saved amount of 12.5, when it is displayed in the expense list and summary, then it is shown as 12.50.\n\n### REQ-009 Fast local response (should)\n\nPages and summaries respond quickly on the local machine, including with a large number of stored expenses.\n\n- Given 10,000 stored expenses, when the user opens the summary page, then the page is displayed within 1 second.\n- Given 10,000 stored expenses, when the user saves a new expense, then the confirmation is displayed within 1 second.\n\n### REQ-010 Clear, usable feedback (should)\n\nThe interface labels every input and shows plain-language messages for validation errors and for saved changes, so the user always knows whether an action succeeded.\n\n- Given any form in the app, when it is displayed, then every field has a visible label stating what it is.\n- Given a failed submission, when the error is shown, then the message names the field and the reason and the entered values remain in the form.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- Single user: no accounts, login or multi-user separation; anyone with local access to the running app sees the same data.\n- The server listens on the local machine only (default port 8000) and the app is started with `python -m expensetracker`.\n- Budgets are monthly: each category has one budget amount that applies to every calendar month and is not copied or reset per month.\n- \"Getting close to the limit\" means 80% of the category budget; over budget means more than 100%.\n- Categories are a fixed list seeded at first run (Groceries, Rent, Transport, Utilities, Entertainment, Health, Other); adding custom categories is not included.\n- A single currency is used, shown without a currency selector; amounts are stored as integer cents.\n- Dates are entered as YYYY-MM-DD and default to today's date in the form.\n- The summary covers only the current calendar month; browsing past months is not included.\n- Editing and deleting saved expenses or budgets is not included in this version, since it was not requested.\n- Storage is a SQLite file in a local data directory next to the package, created automatically on first run.\n"
